<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antananarivo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tananarive</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antananarivo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, in the central highlands. Traditionally said to have been taken by the Merina king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianjaka">Andrianjaka</a> in the early seventeenth century, it became the capital of the reunited Merina kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a> and the seat of its royal palace, the Rova. French forces captured it in 1895, and it remained the capital under colonial rule and after independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antananarivo.jpg" alt="Antananarivo" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sascha Grabow www.saschagrabow.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Anosy,_Central_Antananarivo,_Capital_of_Madagascar,_Photo_by_Sascha_Grabow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Antananarivo stands about 1,280 metres above sea level in the Central Highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, 160 kilometres from the east coast and 330 kilometres from the west coast. The city occupies the summit and slopes of a long, narrow, rocky ridge about 4 kilometres in length, rising some 200 metres above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields to the west. It was originally a town called Analamanga, &quot;Blue Forest&quot;, established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vazimba">Vazimba</a>, considered the island's first occupants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to oral history, the Merina King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianjaka">Andrianjaka</a>, who had migrated from the south-east coast, took the site with a garrison of 1,000 soldiers, and the city later took its name, Antananarivo, &quot;City of the Thousand&quot;, from them under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona">Andriamasinavalona</a>. Andrianjaka built a rova, a fortified royal compound, that grew to enclose the royal palaces and the tombs of Merina royalty. Accounts date the founding to around 1610 or 1625. Early Merina kings used statute labour to build a system of irrigated paddy fields and dikes around the city, of which the largest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsimitatatra">Betsimitatatra</a>, still produces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>.</p><p>By 1810, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> came to the throne, Antananarivo was the largest and most economically important city on the island, with over 80,000 inhabitants. Radama admitted the first European settlers, artisan missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a>, who arrived in 1820 and opened the city's first public schools. His conquests brought nearly two-thirds of the island under his control, and the Merina sovereigns declared Antananarivo the capital of the whole island. Between 1829 and 1842, through war, forced labour, disease and harsh justice, the population of Imerina fell from 750,000 to 130,000. In the last years of the kingdom, most of the city's 50,000 to 75,000 people were slaves, largely captured in provincial campaigns.</p><p>The French military invaded Antananarivo in September 1894, and the queen surrendered after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shell blasted a hole through a building at the Rova. The French made it the capital of their colony, writing the name as Tananarive. They built the Governor General's Residency at Antaninarenina, later the presidential offices, cut tunnels through two of the largest hills, and brought in water from the Ikopa River, sewers and electricity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Antananarivo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, with 1,274,225 inhabitants at the 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and an urban area of almost 2.3 million. It is home to the Presidency, National Assembly, Senate and Supreme Court. The royal compound's largest building, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manjakamiadana">Manjakamiadana</a> palace, stands at the summit of the hills; a fire in 1995 left only its stone casing, which was restored by 2009. Before the mid-19th century houses were built of wood, grasses and reeds, and only family tombs were of stone, a material thought fitting for the dead.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antananarivo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Merina, the people of the central highlands of Madagascar around <b>Antananarivo</b>, descend from the island's Austronesian settlers, whose ancestors probably crossed the Indian Ocean from the region of Borneo during the first millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The treaty of December 1885 gave France control over Madagascar's foreign relations, the bay of Diego-Suarez and a resident-general at <b>Antananarivo</b>, though the Merina government kept internal power.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-madagascar">French Protectorate of Madagascar</a></li></ul></section>
